# `reset-e2e-records.mjs` completeness — evidence for #96 round 3

`reset-e2e-records.mjs` is what both new hooks run to restore "clean shop
records". Against `apps/api/src/db/schema.ts` it was resetting 14 of the 17
shop-record tables and leaving `supplier_account_entries`, `supplier_returns`
and `supplier_return_lines` behind. The rows become orphans once `suppliers` and
`invoices` are deleted, so no assertion sees them — until a later spec reads a
supplier total over the whole database, which is the class this round is about.

The 96 spec's first test records a purchase against an inline supplier, which
writes a supplier-account charge, so it is the right probe.

## Command shape

```
bunx playwright test tests/e2e/96-returns-supplier-action.spec.ts
bun -e '<readonly row counts on .e2e-data/dokan.db>'
```

## BEFORE — the three `DELETE`s removed from the script

```
2 passed (32.0s)
supplier_account_entries 1
supplier_returns 0
supplier_return_lines 0
invoices 0
suppliers 0
returns 0
items 0
```

One orphaned account entry survives the reset. (The two `supplier_return*`
tables are 0 in this probe because the 96 spec records a *customer* return;
`supplier-account.spec.ts` is the spec that writes supplier returns, which is
why they are fixed too and not only the table that showed a row here.)

## AFTER — the three `DELETE`s present

```
2 passed (35.9s)
supplier_account_entries 0
supplier_returns 0
supplier_return_lines 0
invoices 0
suppliers 0
returns 0
items 0
```

The shop records return to zero, which is what the reset's own contract claimed.
